Ready to get Highstate running on your machine?
Right now it's a local development tool - cloud hosting and managed deployments are coming later.

::steps{level="2"}

## Prerequisites

Before installing Highstate, ensure you have the following tools installed:

- [**Bun**](https://bun.sh/) - Package manager and runtime used by Highstate components
- [**Docker**](https://docker.com/) - The default runtime for packaged operations, evaluation, terminals, workers, and image builds
- [**Pulumi CLI**](https://www.pulumi.com/docs/install/) - Used by local development execution; packaged runners include their own toolchain

Some components may require additional tools depending on their functionality.

## Runtime and Pulumi Setup

Highstate uses Pulumi as its infrastructure engine.
Project settings select workload placement separately from Pulumi state storage.
Packaged execution requires a reachable TLS runtime listener and a digest-pinned compatible runner base image.
Configure these before deploying, following [Runtimes and Project Libraries](/advanced/runtime-and-libraries).

The default Pulumi state backend uses persistent storage on the selected runtime.
Other backend URLs can target Pulumi Cloud, S3, Azure Blob, or Google Cloud Storage.
Set the backend URL and authentication in Highstate's Pulumi state backend settings, then select it for the project.
Running `pulumi login` on the backend host does not configure a containerized runner.

For more details, see [Pulumi's state backend documentation](https://www.pulumi.com/docs/concepts/state/).

## Create New Project

The easiest way to create a project is to use the scaffolding command:

```bash
# create a new Highstate project
bun create highstate --name highstate-project --path highstate-project

# enter the project
cd highstate-project
```

The generated project includes the Highstate CLI, the contract package, the Pulumi integration, and the Standard Library.
Project libraries resolve exact npm versions into snapshots whose dependencies are installed into execution images.

## Launch Highstate Designer

```bash
# launch Highstate designer from project directory
highstate designer
```

Then you can open the designer in you browser by provided URL and create your first project.
Highstate stores its local data in `.highstate/`, keeps project models in `projects/`, and uses the configured Pulumi backend for Pulumi state.
The project library view shows snapshot resolution and image-build status before packaged operations can run.

::

